---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/data/site';
import { url } from '@/lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Machine HMI — ind-ds"
  description="Build compact, touch-friendly machine HMIs with ind-ds: machine modes, start/stop, setpoints, alarms, equipment state and connection status — often on an offline embedded panel."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Use case"
    title="Machine HMI"
    lead="A single machine or skid usually has one operator, one screen and little room for error. ind-ds gives that screen explicit operating modes, controls, setpoints and equipment state instead of generic UI widgets."
    crumbs={[
      { label: 'Home', href: url('/') },
      { label: 'Use cases', href: url('/use-cases') },
      { label: 'Machine HMI' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack uc-detail">
      <h2>Typical environment</h2>
      <p>
        A fixed panel mounted on or near the machine — frequently an embedded Chromium display on an
        industrial PC or a WebView on a panel PC. Screens are small (7–15 inch is common), operated by
        gloved hands or a resistive touch layer, and are often expected to keep working when the wider
        network is unavailable. Lighting varies from dim enclosures to bright factory floors, so the
        dark and high-contrast themes both matter.
      </p>

      <h2>User roles</h2>
      <p>
        The primary role is a machine operator who starts, stops and adjusts the machine and responds
        to alarms. Secondary roles include a line supervisor checking mode and throughput, and a
        maintenance technician reading equipment state and recent faults. The interface should be
        readable and safe for all three without a manual.
      </p>

      <h2>Interface challenges</h2>
      <p>
        Touch targets must be large and unambiguous. Machine mode (for example manual, automatic,
        maintenance) has to be visible at a glance and hard to change by accident. Start, stop and
        emergency-stop affordances must be distinct. Setpoints need clear engineering units and safe
        entry. Alarms must stand out without turning the whole screen into noise, and connection loss
        must be obvious rather than silently showing stale values.
      </p>

      <h2>How ind-ds helps</h2>
      <p>
        The control components map directly to these needs. <code>ind-mode-selector</code> renders the
        current operating mode and lets the operator switch it deliberately.
        <code>ind-start-stop-control</code> and <code>ind-estop</code> provide the primary machine
        actions with consistent, high-visibility styling. <code>ind-setpoint-control</code> handles
        target entry with units, and <code>ind-virtual-keyboard</code> supports numeric input on a
        touch-only panel. Equipment symbols such as <code>ind-pump</code>, <code>ind-valve</code> and
        <code>ind-motor</code> carry the semantic states running, stopped, fault, warning and
        maintenance, while <code>ind-value</code>, <code>ind-gauge</code> and
        <code>ind-linear-gauge</code> show live process readings. <code>ind-alarm-panel</code> and
        <code>ind-alarm-summary</code> surface ISA-18.2-aware priorities (HH/H/L/LL) with acknowledge
        and timestamps, and <code>ind-connection-indicator</code> makes link status explicit.
      </p>

      <h2>Example composition</h2>
      <p>A compact single-machine panel might be described as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Top bar: <code>ind-app-header</code> with machine name, plus <code>ind-status-bar</code> and <code>ind-connection-indicator</code>.</li>
        <li>Left: an equipment mimic using <code>ind-pump</code>, <code>ind-valve</code> and <code>ind-tank</code> with live states.</li>
        <li>Centre: <code>ind-mode-selector</code>, <code>ind-start-stop-control</code>, <code>ind-setpoint-control</code> and a prominent <code>ind-estop</code>.</li>
        <li>Right: <code>ind-value</code> and <code>ind-linear-gauge</code> readouts, with <code>ind-sparkline</code> for recent trend.</li>
        <li>Bottom: <code>ind-alarm-summary</code>, expanding to <code>ind-alarm-panel</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These are named for illustration; the components are not rendered live on this page.</p>

      <h2>Integration approach</h2>
      <p>
        Use the Web Components directly, or the typed React (18+) or Vue (3.3+) wrappers if the panel
        app already uses one of those frameworks. Live values can be bound with the
        <code>@ind-ds/mqtt</code> helpers, which map broker topics to component attributes. The helpers
        bind data to the DOM; they are not a broker, PLC driver or historian, so the application still
        owns data acquisition, security and network segmentation between the control network and the
        panel.
      </p>

      <h2>Adoption strategy</h2>
      <p>
        Start by adopting the tokens so the panel matches your visual language, then replace the most
        semantic elements first — equipment states, alarms and the mode selector — before migrating
        the rest of the screen. A single machine is a good pilot because its scope is bounded and the
        feedback loop with operators is short.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        ind-ds does not replace PLC programming, safety engineering or alarm rationalisation. An
        <code>ind-estop</code> in the browser is an interface element, not a certified safety function;
        real emergency stop must be hardwired and engineered to the applicable standards. Offline
        operation depends on how you bundle and cache the app and on your local data source — the
        components render offline, but they cannot invent data the panel can no longer reach. Choosing
        the high-contrast theme is one input to accessibility, not a compliance guarantee.
      </p>

      <div class="panel uc-cta">
        <a href={url('/components')} class="btn btn--default">Explore components</a>
        <a href={url('/getting-started')} class="btn btn--ghost">Get started</a>
        <a href={SITE.storybook} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--ghost">Open Storybook →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .uc-detail h2 { margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
  .uc-detail p { max-width: 68ch; }
  .uc-detail ul { max-width: 68ch; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: var(--ind-spacing-2); }
  .uc-detail code { font-family: var(--ind-font-family-mono); font-size: 0.9em; }
  .uc-cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ind-spacing-3); margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-8); padding: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
</style>
